# weather.gen -- nothing here plays a note on purpose.
#
# Four of the five chains emit no pitches at all. They are random walks,
# pointed at knobs: the filter opens and closes over minutes, the
# resonance wanders under it, the cutoff floor breathes on a period that
# shares no factor with either. What you hear is one very simple pad
# being weathered.
#
# This is the plainest form of the thing tide.gen and bloom.gen do with
# presets. A walk emits a bare number and does not know or care which
# knob it lands on -- routing belongs to the piece, so the *sink* names
# the target, and pointing the same walk somewhere else is a one-word
# edit. Presets exist for when a composer wants to move several knobs at
# once as a vector; a single knob wants nothing more than this.
#
# The walk reflects off its bounds rather than clamping, so it does not
# stick to an edge the way a clamped walk does, and the three periods are
# deliberately incommensurate -- 17, 23.5 and 31.7 seconds -- so the
# three curves never line up twice. That is airports.gen's trick applied
# to timbre instead of to melody.
#
# The Weather knob scales how far every walk may step, so one slider runs
# from "almost still" to "restless" across all three at once. That is a
# knob bound into three stages, which is what a live binding is for: the
# param store resolves it, and nothing in the plugin knows.
#
# The seed is pinned, so the weather repeats. Unpin it for a different
# afternoon each time.
#
# Aim channel 1 at amb01.dsp, which declares res, fmin and fmax. Any
# patch that declares none of them will simply play the pad dry: the
# chanargs a patch exposes are the whole of what a piece may reach.

name "Weather";
author "Misha Nasledov";
description "Three random walks, one pad, and no notes worth mentioning.";

seed 5090;

@weather = 0.05;
@weather.widget = 1;
@weather.min = 0;
@weather.max = 0.3;
@weather.label = "Weather";

scale dmin "D3 E3 F3 A3 C4 D4";

chain pad {
    stage src gen::eno_line {
        notes = dmin;
        period = 14.5 s; jitter = 3.5 s;
        prob = 0.7; hold = 13 s; vel = 54; vel_jitter = 6;
    };
    sink { channel = 1; };
};

chain ceiling {
    stage src gen::walk {
        min = 0.25; max = 0.98;
        step = @weather;
        period = 17 s;
    };
    sink { channel = 1; chanarg = "fmax"; };
};

chain floor {
    stage src gen::walk {
        min = 0.01; max = 0.22;
        step = @weather;
        period = 23.5 s;
    };
    sink { channel = 1; chanarg = "fmin"; };
};

chain ring {
    stage src gen::walk {
        min = 0.15; max = 0.95;
        step = @weather;
        period = 31.7 s;
    };
    sink { channel = 1; chanarg = "res"; };
};

# One walk, two destinations. Every event leaving the chain is delivered
# to each sink, so the same drift lands on two patches at once -- which is
# what fan-out is for, and why the type filtering lives in the sink
# rather than in the stage.
chain shared {
    stage src gen::walk {
        min = 0.3; max = 0.9;
        step = @weather;
        period = 41.3 s;
    };
    sink { channel = 1; chanarg = "amp"; };
    sink { channel = 2; chanarg = "amp"; };
};
